    <div class="lang-en">
      <header>
        <p class="eyebrow">Guide &amp; documentation</p>
        <h1>Designing forest gardens with the Perma&nbsp;Design&nbsp;Kit</h1>
        <p class="lead">A digital planning tool for permanent food plant systems – a design tool for laying out forest gardens.</p>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="en-so-planst-du" class="intro">
        <h2 id="en-so-planst-du">How to plan</h2>
        <figure class="hero">
          <a href="/hilfe/planspiel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/planspiel.jpg" width="1400" height="927" decoding="async" alt="A garden outline taped onto a wooden floor with masking tape. Inside lie many colourful round canopy cards, boxes as buildings, next to it an info board and paper fans for the sectors." /></a>
          <figcaption>The first version of the planning tool in action: base map of masking tape, canopy cards at 1:50, boxes as buildings, info board and sectors.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="ways">
          <div class="way">
            <h3>On the floor</h3>
            <ol>
              <li><b>Know your goal and site.</b> Be clear about what you want to achieve. Observe sun, wind, water, soil and surroundings; research and record the site factors.</li>
              <li><b>Plant list.</b> Import an existing list or search for the species you want under <i>Plants</i> and let the app complete the data.</li>
              <li><b>Print canopy cards.</b> In the card view print the true-to-scale PDF (1:50), cut out and adapt.</li>
              <li><b>Make a base map.</b> Transfer the site boundary at the same scale onto any surface, with the site data laid out clearly next to it.</li>
              <li><b>Place and move.</b> By layers: trees first, then shrubs, then perennials, climbers last. Photograph the drafts and keep improving until every plant has a good spot.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="way">
            <h3>In the forest garden plan</h3>
            <ol>
              <li><b>Draw the area.</b> Search an address and outline the garden on the map. Size, north and terrain follow from that.</li>
              <li><b>Place plants.</b> Pick a plant and tap the area – one at a time, as a row or as a whole polyculture.</li>
              <li><b>Let it grow and check.</b> Let it grow with the years slider in 2D or 3D, check the sunlight and close gaps in the functions.</li>
            </ol>
            <figure>
              <a href="/hilfe/waldgartenplan-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/waldgartenplan-en.jpg" width="1400" height="971" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Screenshot of the forest garden plan with the Dartington example in 3D: trees and shrubs on the site, buildings and forest behind, the toolbar on the left, the layers on the right, the years slider and sun position below." /></a>
              <figcaption>The same idea on screen: the Dartington example garden in 3D with terrain, buildings and forest.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="bridge"><b>Combine both:</b> lay out and discuss together on the floor, then transfer the result into the forest garden plan, let it grow there, print and share it. Your data stays on your device.</p>
      </section>


      <details class="part" id="en-idee">
        <summary><span class="num">1</span><span><span class="title">Idea &amp; concept</span><span class="hint">Why canopy cards on a base map</span></span></summary>
        <div class="part-body">
          <p>The Perma Design Kit grew out of the search for a flexible way to plan multi-layered forest gardens. Drawn plans are rigid and tedious to change. On a screen it is easier, but the sense of space and planning together get lost. Hence the idea of joining both worlds: canopy cards that characterise a plant and its traits are placed on a base map of the site, staked out beforehand at a tangible scale – playfully and as a team. Each card carries the key data on the plant’s ecological niche, so while placing it you keep in view what it needs and what it gives.</p>
          <p>At the heart of it is the <b>set of canopy cards</b>, each showing the crown diameter of the mature plant at the chosen scale. The software lets you create and print such a set at any scale.</p>
          <p>Below you find <a href="#analog">how to use the software for this</a> and <a href="#software">what else it offers</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="part" id="en-anwendung">
        <summary><span class="num">2</span><span><span class="title">Uses</span><span class="hint">Site design, planning together, education</span></span></summary>
        <div class="part-body">
          <div class="split">
            <div>
              <h3>Site design</h3>
              <p>The Perma Design Kit is a visual aid for site design, more precisely for placing elements (plants) concretely. It makes for better design in the sense of positioning elements as well as possible in relation to each other (“knowing what goes where”). The software produces the analogue canopy cards for printing; the site design can also be done digitally in the <a href="#gartenplan">forest garden plan</a>.</p>
              <p>Either way it is a tool that works in a group too and lets people plan shared land together.</p>
            </div>
            <figure>
              <a href="/hilfe/gemeinschaftsgarten.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/gemeinschaftsgarten.jpg" width="1400" height="1400" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Two people kneel on the floor and place coloured round canopy cards on a long plan made of paper strips, a laptop next to them." /></a>
              <figcaption>Planning a community forest garden.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
          <h3>Education</h3>
          <p>The Perma Design Kit also works as a teaching and learning aid for all approaches to land use with trees. It conveys principles of site design playfully and trains the imagination for the dynamic webs of relationships in space and time that mark permanent systems.</p>
          <p>Building and editing plant lists teaches specialised knowledge of ecological niches along the way. Creating and personalising a set of true-to-scale canopy cards has a particularly strong learning effect.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="part" id="en-analog">
        <summary><span class="num">3</span><span><span class="title">The analogue tool</span><span class="hint">Site analysis, base map, reading and printing canopy cards, placing plants</span></span></summary>
        <div class="part-body">
          <p>This is how the tool works as an analogue site design aid – the five steps are listed above under <a href="#so-planst-du">How to plan</a>, here in detail.</p>

          <details class="sub" id="en-standort"><summary>From site analysis to base map</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>Usually you work on a specific site you know more or less about.</p>
            <h4>Gathering site data</h4>
            <p>Observation on site and research give you a collection of data on the site’s <b>conditions and surroundings</b>: general climate and microclimate, history of use, soils, existing plants and animals and much more.</p>
            <p>Permaculture analysis methods such as <b>sector analysis</b> open further perspectives on that data. They show influences acting on the site from outside: the sun’s path over the year, wind, noise, views in and out. In residential settings <b>zoning</b> is useful too, dividing the site by how often and how intensively it is used.</p>
            <figure class="narrow">
              <a href="/hilfe/sektoren.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/sektoren.jpg" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Fans of coloured paper labelled view, traffic noise, neighbours and noise." /></a>
              <figcaption>Sector data made visible the simple way.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <h4>Making the base map</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Take the site boundary from your own plans, public cadastral maps or architects’ scale drawings and draw the site at the scale of the printed cards (1:50 by default). As with drawn plans, an accurate scale matters for finding the right planting distances.</li>
              <li>There are many ways to do this: stake it out in a sandpit, tape it onto the floor with masking tape, draw it with chalk on a wall or a large surface – magnetic boards, pinboards, large posters, furniture boxes or rolls of wallpaper.</li>
              <li>Surrounding structures such as buildings and hedges can be shown with objects, e.g. boxes. With a movable light source their shade becomes tangible.</li>
              <li>To keep the site conditions present at all times, the analysis results are shown in simplified form – as an info sheet or, as here, an “info board”.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="figs">
              <figure>
                <a href="/hilfe/grundkarte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/grundkarte.jpg" width="1400" height="927" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The outline of a site taped onto a wooden floor with masking tape, two wooden blocks as buildings and an info board inside." /></a>
                <figcaption>The base map taped onto the floor with masking tape.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <a href="/hilfe/infoboard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/infoboard.jpg" width="1400" height="927" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Wooden board with a rotating sun disc, north arrow, wind arrow and colour scales for rainfall and hardiness zone." /></a>
                <figcaption>The key site factors at a glance: sun path, annual rainfall, soil type, prevailing wind, hardiness zone.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
            <figure class="narrow">
              <a href="/hilfe/szenario.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/szenario.jpg" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Info bar “Scenario C: allotment amid fields” with site size 200 × 50 m, north arrow, 800 mm rainfall and USDA zone 6, below it a short description." /></a>
              <figcaption>A rough overview as the basis for a site scenario.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-baumscheibe"><summary>From plant list to a set of canopy cards</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>All card views aim to show a plant’s <b>ecological niche</b>, so that placing it rests on the best possible information. The key information is on the front: names, dimensions, needs and tolerances, uses and functions. The back is free for further notes.</p>
            <div class="split">
              <figure>
                <a href="/hilfe/baumscheibe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/baumscheibe.jpg" width="1400" height="1427" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Canopy card of black locust (Robinia pseudoacacia): name and rating at the top, below height 25 m, crown 15 m, hardiness 4–9, coloured fields for functions and uses at the bottom, month rings around the edge." /></a>
                <figcaption>A canopy card, using black locust as the example.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <div>
                <h4>The canopy card in detail</h4>
                <dl>
                  <dt>Very top</dt><dd>The <b>rating</b> as a useful plant, below it in the white field the <b>botanical and common name</b>. The symbol on the left shows the <b>layer</b>.</dd>
                  <dt>Grey band</dt><dd>The data that matter most for placing (see below).</dd>
                  <dt>Lower half</dt><dd>The two inner circles show the <b>functions</b> in the ecosystem, the outer one the <b>yields</b> for people, mostly products (see <a href="#platzieren">Placing the plants</a>).</dd>
                  <dt>Rings</dt><dd>Outside the <b>flowering time</b>, inside the <b>ripening and harvest time</b>, each anticlockwise from January to December.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </div>
            <h4>The grey band</h4>
            <dl>
              <dt>Growth</dt><dd>slow – moderate – fast growing</dd>
              <dt>Height</dt><dd>maximum height of the mature plant</dd>
              <dt>Crown diameter</dt><dd>diameter of the mature crown, or spread for perennials</dd>
              <dt>Soil pH</dt><dd>acid to neutral, neutral, neutral to alkaline</dd>
              <dt>Hardiness</dt><dd>USDA zone</dd>
              <dt>Light</dt><dd>the plant’s preference (optimum) from full sun through part shade to full shade</dd>
              <dt>Water</dt><dd>water need combined with the soil’s drainage or water-holding capacity (free-draining, moist, wet)</dd>
              <dt>Soil <span class="soon">soon</span></dt><dd>sand – silt – loam – centre. If all four symbols are shown, the plant suits every soil.</dd>
            </dl>
            <h4>Printing the set</h4>
            <p>In the card view under <code>True-to-scale PDF</code>. Each disc gets the crown width at the chosen scale, laid out on A4, A3 or A2.</p>
            <div class="split">
              <dl>
                <dt>Scale 1:50</dt><dd>The default. Covers medium to large sites, and the set stays handy. 4 m crown = 8 cm disc.</dd>
                <dt>Min. size 4 cm</dt><dd>Smaller discs are not printed because they would hardly be readable. The app tells you which are missing.</dd>
                <dt>Fixed 6 cm + ring</dt><dd>Every info circle is 6 cm, the outer ring shows the real crown size.</dd>
              </dl>
              <figure>
                <a href="/hilfe/baumscheiben-druck-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/baumscheiben-druck-en.jpg" width="1051" height="725" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Screenshot of the card view with the “PDF (scale)” menu open: scale 1:50, paper A4, minimum size 4 cm and the option “fixed 6 cm disc + scale ring”." /></a>
                <figcaption>Settings for the true-to-scale PDF.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
            <h4>Making them your own</h4>
            <p>Print in colour or greyscale on paper or film and cut out the circles. It pays to adapt the prints to your situation and your own way of seeing: coloured paper or colouring in makes the discs easy to tell apart, and the back has room for more information.</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-platzieren"><summary>From site draft to planting plan</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <h4>Placing the plants</h4>
            <p>Where exactly a plant goes depends above all on the site conditions and the plant’s traits. So this information should always be present.</p>
            <p>The simplest way to show the <b>site analysis</b> is a sheet with the key data. At the other end of the scale is an “info board” that shows north and the sun’s path plus rainfall, prevailing wind, hardiness zone and soil type. In our latitudes light is the limiting factor for systems that are productive on every level – so the sun’s path is the most important information for positioning individual plants.</p>
            <p>The <b>plant traits</b> that matter for placing are on the canopy cards. Their functions are colour-coded, so the basic conditions for a polyculture are quickly visible – <b>polyculture design by colours</b>:</p>
            <div class="tip"><b>Basic recipe</b>Every colour appears at least once in a patch, better twice. That lays the foundation. With some stacking of functions and redundancy within the patch you are quickly on the right track (see <a href="#polykulturen">Polycultures &amp; guilds</a>).</div>
            <figure>
              <a href="/hilfe/legende-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/legende-en.jpg" width="1400" height="1720" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Explanatory graphic “plant information” (in German): a canopy card with every field labelled, below it the colour legend of functions for the ecosystem and uses for people." /></a>
              <figcaption>Legend of plant information, functions and uses (German).</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <h4>Documentation</h4>
            <p>With the analogue set, photos are the best way to record drafts. Clear, consistent file names keep the results in order. If you want to develop the drafts further professionally, transfer them into the digital <a href="#gartenplan">forest garden plan</a>, which offers features similar to a geographic information system (GIS).</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-grenzen"><summary>Current limits</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <h4>Readability of the cards</h4>
            <p>At the default scale of 1:50 only woody plants and large enough perennials (from about 1 m across) show well; on smaller circles the data is at the edge of readability. A variant with abbreviations instead of symbols is being tested and can help (<code>Settings → View → Abbreviations</code> instead of colour dots).</p>
            <p>The early phases of planting show very well at the default scale: tree and shrub layers usually go in first, the herbaceous layers follow in later years.</p>
            <h4>Showing guilds</h4>
            <p>Whether all ecosystem functions are covered for all plants can only be judged to a limited extent so far. For single polycultures there is a provisional solution: irregularly cut-out shapes that stand for groups of herbaceous plants. Their information can be shown outside the plan, e.g. with the strip view.</p>
            <div class="figs">
              <figure>
                <a href="/hilfe/gilden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/gilden.jpg" width="1400" height="927" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Canopy card of black mulberry on a large piece of cardboard, next to it two green irregular cut-outs for pollinator plants and scent confusers." /></a>
                <figcaption>Cut-out shapes for the guilds of a polyculture.</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <figure>
                <a href="/hilfe/kraeuterschicht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/kraeuterschicht.jpg" width="875" height="628" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Turquoise irregular card “herb layer patch” listing herbs with coloured dots for their functions." /></a>
                <figcaption>A card for the herb layer of a patch.</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>
            <p>A guild is not the same as a polyculture, though, because its members are not confined to one patch. The pollinator guild, for example, usually consists of flowering plants spread across the whole forest garden. For now we try to cover this complexity in the digital features <i>Polycultures</i> and <i>Forest garden plan</i> (see <a href="#polykulturen">Polycultures &amp; guilds</a>).</p>
          </div></details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="part" id="en-software">
        <summary><span class="num">4</span><span><span class="title">The software: permadesignkit.org</span><span class="hint">Plants, calendar, polycultures, forest garden plan, backups, shortcuts, outlook</span></span></summary>
        <div class="part-body">
          <p>Beyond the physical set of canopy cards, the software offers powerful tools, above all for the design phase of a design process.</p>
          <h3>Technical notes</h3>
          <p>The Perma Design Kit runs <b>in the browser</b>. It gathers data from public databases (mainly PFAF and Wikidata) and prepares it for different views. All imported data and your own entries are stored only in the browser and never leave your computer – until the browser’s storage is cleared. How to back them up is described under <a href="#daten">Backup, sharing, offline use</a>.</p>
          <p>The tool should work in all common browsers and on mobile devices. If it doesn’t, please <a href="/fehler-melden/">report a bug</a>.</p>
          <div class="warn"><b>Caution</b>The plant data comes from third-party sources and is not always complete or correct. Check it, especially for important decisions.</div>
          <h3>Layout &amp; use</h3>
          <p>On your first visit a tour explains the basics. The menu at the top holds the pages:</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Plants</dt><dd>collecting the niche data and showing it graphically in different views</dd>
            <dt>Calendar</dt><dd>flowering and harvest calendars</dd>
            <dt>Polycultures</dt><dd>putting together functional plant communities</dd>
            <dt>Forest garden plan</dt><dd>laying out a plant list on the real site (in development)</dd>
          </dl>
          <figure>
            <a href="/hilfe/pflanzenkacheln-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/pflanzenkacheln-en.jpg" width="1400" height="815" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Screenshot of the Plants page in tile view: the menu at the top, below filters and plant tiles with photos, names and coloured use badges." /></a>
            <figcaption>The <i>Plants</i> page in tile view.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <details class="sub" id="en-pflanzen"><summary>Plants</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>The core of the app: the collected data on the plants’ ecological niches, filterable and sortable in several views.</p>
            <h4>a) Starting a plant list from scratch</h4>
            <ol>
              <li><b>Add a plant.</b> The search box finds plants in the sources. Set the number of specimens right there. <code>New plant</code> lets you enter everything by hand.</li>
              <li><b>Let the app complete the data.</b> Missing details come from PFAF, Edible Forest Gardens and Wikidata (see <a href="#quellen">data sources</a>).</li>
              <li><b>Correct and edit.</b> Change any value in the edit dialog. The source tag then switches to <b>M</b> for manual, and that value is never overwritten.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="tip"><b>Tip</b>Searching works best with the botanical name; common names are not always unambiguous.</div>
            <p>It is important to adapt the plant data to your own situation: you need your own <b>sizes</b> for varieties, different rootstocks or older existing trees. The same goes when the size depends on management, e.g. trees grown as coppice (lime for salad leaves, hazel for poles, willow for basketry). Varieties also often have different <b>flowering and ripening times</b>.</p>
            <h4>b) Using existing lists</h4>
            <p>Read in ready-made plant lists as CSV or JSON via the <code>Data</code> menu. An example list, “Plants for small gardens” (42 species), is available there. Export tables from Excel as CSV and import them here.</p>
            <h4>Views</h4>
            <p>Every view has the <code>Data</code> button: complete data, import and export lists, create print views. The filter row sorts and filters the plants.</p>
            <figure class="narrow">
              <img src="/hilfe/menue-en.jpg" width="448" height="101" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Header “Pflanzenkacheln” (plant tiles) with the view switch for tiles, list and cards." />
            </figure>
            <dl>
              <dt>Tiles</dt><dd>Sortable, filterable overview with image, accent stripe by main use and completeness bar.</dd>
              <dt>List</dt><dd>Sortable, filterable table. Select several plants and complete, delete, export or print them together.</dd>
              <dt>Canopy cards</dt><dd>A further view alongside the tiles. The print buttons create the true-to-scale canopy cards as a PDF.</dd>
            </dl>
            <h4>Symbols in the plant list</h4>
            <ul class="legend">
              <li><b>Filled badges:</b> the plant’s use (edible, medicinal, material …).</li>
              <li><b>Outlined chips:</b> where a value came from, e.g. PFAF.</li>
              <li><b>Accent stripe on the left:</b> main use in this order:
                <span class="swatches"><span><i class="sw" style="background:#fbbf24"></i>edible/culinary</span><span><i class="sw" style="background:#fb7185"></i>medicinal</span><span><i class="sw" style="background:#84cc16"></i>nitrogen fixer</span><span><i class="sw" style="background:#2dd4bf"></i>ground cover</span><span><i class="sw" style="background:#22d3ee"></i>insect plant</span></span></li>
              <li><b>Data completeness:</b> green from 80 %, yellow from 50 %, red below.</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Editing</h4>
            <p>Open any plant for editing with the <code>pencil</code> button to fill in missing data and adapt it to your garden.</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-kalender"><summary>Calendar</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>Flowering and harvest periods as a calendar, sortable and printable as a PDF.</p>
            <figure>
              <a href="/hilfe/kalender-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/kalender-en.jpg" width="1400" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Screenshot of the flowering and harvest calendar: a monthly overview with bars at the top, below it flowering and harvest as coloured bars across twelve months for each plant." /></a>
              <figcaption>Flowering and harvest calendar.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-polykulturen-app"><summary>Polycultures</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>This tool suggests planting partners with similar needs and tolerances for light, water and soil pH. Saved polycultures can be placed in the forest garden plan as a unit. <span class="soon">first draft, in development</span></p>
            <h4>Creating polycultures</h4>
            <ol>
              <li>Choose an anchor species, often a central element such as a fruit tree.</li>
              <li>Add members, filling each role at least twice: companion, ground cover, nitrogen fixer, mineral accumulator, insect or scent plant, fruit or berry (see <a href="#polykulturen">design principles for polycultures</a>).</li>
              <li>The suggestion panel on the right shows matching plants from your list and further suggestions – the higher the number, the better light, water and pH match.</li>
              <li>Save.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>Alternatively create a <b>group</b> in a plant’s edit dialog and turn it into a polyculture with <code>Create from group</code>. Ready-made example polycultures are in the page menu and in the <a href="#literatur">sources</a>.</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-gartenplan"><summary>Forest garden plan</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>The digital alternative to the set of canopy cards. This is where most development is still needed: a digital tool ultimately has to be complex enough for professional planning and compatible with GIS applications.</p>
            <div class="beta">
              <b>Beta test</b>
              <p>We are working out quite a few things. The tool already has many new features that are not public yet and want testing, among them:</p>
              <ul>
                <li>tree biographies and tasks: a timeline for every tree with assessments, harvest, pruning, care and photos</li>
                <li>better integration with (Q)GIS</li>
                <li>working on plans together as a team and sharing them</li>
                <li>a professional forest garden planner with layers, harvest gap analysis, windbreak, water flow and more</li>
              </ul>
              <p>If you’d like to try the new features in the <a href="/beta/">beta test</a>: we look forward to your feedback, your support and your advice on where the tool should go. Maybe you even feel like joining in and becoming part of the team?</p>
            </div>
            <p>At the top right you choose how extensive the <b>interface</b> is. <b>Simple</b> walks you through the plan in three steps and shows five layers to fill – enough for most gardens. <b>Classic</b> shows all tools with a professional feature set. You can switch at any time; it only changes the interface, not the data in the plan. Independently of that, every plan can be viewed in <b>2D or 3D</b>.</p>
            <h4>Creating a site plan and placing plants</h4>
            <ul>
              <li><code>+ New forest garden plan</code>, search an address or choose <i>My location</i>, and click the corners of the site. Size, north and terrain follow from that. Without a location you get a plain grid.</li>
              <li><i>Draw area</i> for parts such as beds, pond and paths.</li>
              <li><b>Placing plants:</b> pick a plant and click the area – one at a time, as a row at a fixed spacing or as a whole polyculture (<i>Polyculture as package</i> places anchor and members at once).</li>
              <li>Drag plants to move them, select several at once with the <i>lasso</i>. Dragging on empty ground pans the map.</li>
              <li>The <i>years since project start</i> slider (0–80) shows the estimated crown size in each year. Fast-growing plants are mature after about 6, medium ones after 15, slow ones after 30 years. Growth form and habit are shown in a simple standard way for now.</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Map, aerial photo, terrain</h4>
            <p>The background comes from public data: OpenStreetMap, official aerial photos (8–40 cm, in 13 German states, Austria, Switzerland and the Netherlands) or a coarse satellite image. Terrain comes from official 1 m models where available. Buildings within about 150 m stand in 3D and cast shade.</p>
            <h4>Analyses</h4>
            <dl>
              <dt>Functions</dt><dd>Effective guilds for the whole system: where the seven ecosystem functions act and where they are missing, with suggestions from your collection and <i>Plant here</i>.</dd>
              <dt>Sunlight</dt><dd>Hours of sun per day with shade from tree crowns, terrain and buildings: sun from 6 h, part shade 3–6 h, shade below 3 h.</dd>
              <dt>Hints</dt><dd>(experimental) Possible conflicts from plants of the same layer standing too close and unmet light needs, each for mature plants.</dd>
            </dl>
            <h4>Tools and output</h4>
            <div class="cols">
              <ul>
                <li>several selection modes</li>
                <li>measuring</li>
                <li>saving an image</li>
                <li>drawing areas and outlines</li>
                <li>GPS data</li>
              </ul>
              <ul>
                <li>plant list with quantities and prices (CSV, PDF)</li>
                <li>calendar for this plan only</li>
                <li>PDF export</li>
                <li>GeoJSON for QGIS</li>
                <li>share link</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-daten"><summary>Backup, sharing, offline use</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <dl>
              <dt>Backup</dt><dd><code>Settings → Data</code>: save all data as a JSON file, also to move to another device. Can run automatically via WebDAV or Gist.</dd>
              <dt>Export</dt><dd>All or selected plants as CSV or JSON.</dd>
              <dt>Share link</dt><dd>Plants, polycultures and plans are packed, compressed, into the link. Nothing is stored on the server.</dd>
              <dt>Install the app</dt><dd>Android/Chrome: “Install app” prompt. iOS: Share icon → “Add to Home Screen”. After the first visit everything runs offline except search, data sources and the map.</dd>
            </dl>
            <div class="warn"><b>Important on iPhone and iPad</b>The app on the home screen has its own, empty storage. Make a backup in the browser first and read it into the app. In a Safari tab iOS may delete data after 7 days without a visit.</div>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-shortcuts"><summary>Keyboard shortcuts</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <div class="tablewrap"><table>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><kbd>/</kbd></td><td>Focus search</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Close the open dialog</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>f</kbd></td><td>Filter the list by name</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>n</kbd></td><td>New plant</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>g</kbd> <kbd>l</kbd> <kbd>c</kbd></td><td>Switch view: tiles, list, cards</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd> + click</td><td>Select a range in the list</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>1</kbd>…<kbd>9</kbd></td><td>Menu items in order</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>?</kbd></td><td>All shortcuts of the current page</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></div>
            <p><b>In the forest garden plan:</b> <kbd>2</kbd>/<kbd>3</kbd> for 2D/3D, <kbd>H</kbd> <kbd>V</kbd> <kbd>L</kbd> <kbd>M</kbd> for pan, select, lasso, measure, <kbd>F</kbd> <kbd>R</kbd> <kbd>U</kbd> for area, row, outline, <kbd>I</kbd>/<kbd>O</kbd> for the years, <kbd>J</kbd>/<kbd>K</kbd> for the sun.</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-ausblick"><summary>Outlook &amp; getting involved</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <h4>Planned development</h4>
            <p>Many more features are planned or already being tested:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>plant lists by design goal: food production systems, complex agroforestry, growing for health and more</li>
              <li>showing different succession phases in the plan</li>
              <li>polycultures and guilds: set your own criteria for suggestions and show guilds in space</li>
              <li>interchangeable uses and functions</li>
              <li>vector files for laser-cutting canopy cards from wood, MDF or plastic</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Get involved &amp; support</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Use the tool and give feedback, report bugs via <a href="/fehler-melden/">report a bug</a></li>
              <li>Create plant lists and polycultures to share</li>
              <li>Contribute ideas and code on GitHub</li>
              <li>Help cover server and domain costs via <a href="/unterstuetzen/">support</a></li>
              <li>Try unreleased features in the <a href="/beta/">beta test</a></li>
            </ul>
          </div></details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="part" id="en-hintergrund">
        <summary><span class="num">5</span><span><span class="title">Background</span><span class="hint">Design process, forest gardens, polycultures &amp; guilds</span></span></summary>
        <div class="part-body">
          <p>For most people the tool works best in its simplified form. But it is designed to go much further and allows far more detail and customisation, so professional planning is possible too. Here we only give a short outline; for more depth see the <a href="#literatur">sources</a> below.</p>
          <p>In general we want to answer the question of how our land use can be shaped in harmony with nature. For this we draw above all on ecology and the natural sciences, applied in the spirit of permaculture ethics and design principles. Most important is observing and imitating nature – from it we learn to “garden like the forest”.</p>

          <details class="sub" id="en-designprozess"><summary>Design process</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>Good design is most likely when you follow a design process. This logical sequence of phases ensures that we know as much as possible about the site before the actual site design begins. The better founded the site design, the more successful and productive the implementation. And the longer we let the process guide us, the better the design gets through repeated loops of evaluation and improvement.</p>
            <figure>
              <a href="/hilfe/designprozess.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/designprozess.jpg" width="622" height="455" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Colourful diagram of a design process for forest gardens with the phases observation, analysis, design, implementation and management around a spiral." /></a>
              <figcaption>A design process for forest gardens.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-waldgaerten"><summary>Forest gardens</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>Unlike intensive growing of annuals, multi-layered permanent systems call for more complex, holistic thinking. Spatially alone we move from the almost two-dimensional vegetable bed into the third dimension. The biggest difference, though, is the fourth dimension: time. Forests are the most efficient ecosystems we can create and shape, so our goal is usually the <b>climax stage of natural succession</b>. What can take a millennium at nature’s pace can be sped up considerably through ecologically informed interventions.</p>
            <p>It helps a lot to “fast-forward” time in your head and picture later stages of succession – to think in world time instead of human time. With some practice a <b>successional imagination</b> develops that opens up the time perspective and allows better decisions about where trees and shrubs go. In the forest garden plan the years slider helps with this.</p>
            <p>For designing forest gardens and complex agroforestry systems we build on the established standard works: “Creating a Forest Garden” by Martin Crawford and the two volumes of “Edible Forest Gardens” by Dave Jacke and Eric Toensmeier (see <a href="#literatur">sources</a>).</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-polykulturen"><summary>Polycultures &amp; guilds</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>Roughly speaking, a forest garden is a collection of polycultures: paths usually divide the site into bounded <i>patches</i> on which multi-layered plant communities grow. Ideally they cover the ecosystem functions their success requires – measured by the yields they are designed for. A fruit tree has the least stress in a community, because it supports and protects it with pollination, pest control, nutrients and ground cover.</p>
            <div class="def"><b>Polyculture</b>A polyculture is defined by physical space: a group of mainly perennial plants on a clearly bounded area (<i>patch</i>), arranged so that their interactions show as little competition and as much cooperation as possible.</div>
            <p>Once you know the plants’ ecological niches, you can arrange them against the background of the site factors so that they deliver the <b>yields</b> you want.</p>
            <figure>
              <a href="/hilfe/nutzen-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/nutzen-en.jpg" width="1231" height="415" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Symbols of uses for humans: edible parts, medicinal, culinary, timber fuel, materials, soap, dyes, aesthetic; below fruit, leaves, greens, flowers, seeds, nuts, bulbs, roots, fungi, oils." /></a>
              <figcaption>Uses for humans.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p>Designing successful polycultures follows these <b>design principles</b>:</p>
            <dl>
              <dt>Relative location</dt><dd>Place elements in relation to each other so that competition is low and cooperation is favoured.</dd>
              <dt>Stacking functions</dt><dd>Each element fulfils as many functions as possible and so builds many relationships.</dd>
              <dt>Redundancy</dt><dd>Each function is fulfilled by several elements, so it is reliably provided for the polyculture.</dd>
            </dl>
            <p>It also always helps to keep <b>permaculture</b> principles in mind: design from patterns to details, use and value diversity, integrate rather than segregate (see Holmgren in the <a href="#literatur">sources</a>).</p>
            <p>Besides their uses for us, polycultures should also provide important <b>functions for the ecosystem</b>.</p>
            <figure>
              <a href="/hilfe/funktionen-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hilfe/funktionen-en.jpg" width="1222" height="269" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Symbols of functions for the ecosystem: nitrogen fixer (N+), mineral accumulator (DA), windbreak, food and protection for insects and small animals, ground cover, pest control." /></a>
              <figcaption>Functions for the ecosystem.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p>Grouping the plants by function and looking at the relationships between them adds a second perspective: that of the whole ecosystem. To grasp this complexity, the work of Dave Jacke and Eric Toensmeier is helpful. They sharpen the term <b>guild</b> to make the effects across the system visible.</p>
            <div class="def"><b>Guild</b>Unlike polycultures, guilds cannot be confined to the area of one polyculture; they represent functions across the system and thus its “social” structure. A guild is defined by the kind of relationships between the elements, e.g. a guild for windbreak, pollination or ground cover. It follows that <b>not all guilds are polycultures</b>, and that <b>to be effective, a polyculture must contain several guilds</b>.</div>
            <p class="small">These concepts are simplified here; more in Dave Jacke’s talks and in “Edible Forest Gardens” (see <a href="#literatur">sources</a>).</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-beispiele"><summary>Examples of polycultures</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>You can already speak of a polyculture when more than one layer is planted in intensive growing. Such <b>mixed cropping</b> (“companion planting”, “interplanting”) can be mutually beneficial, but it does not build complex relationships.</p>
            <p>Those only arise once woody plants join in, usually as the central element. People then often speak of <b>fruit tree guilds</b>: fruit trees underplanted with supporting plants.</p>
            <p>Beyond that, all kinds of combinations of woody and herbaceous plants for <b>special design goals</b> are possible – the only real limit is imagination. A typical example is a windbreak hedge; screening from view or noise can be the desired yield as well. Polycultures can serve as green manure areas for neighbouring intensive beds, provide culinary uses near the kitchen, supply natural materials for arts and crafts, and much more.</p>
          </div></details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="part" id="en-literatur">
        <summary><span class="num">6</span><span><span class="title">Sources</span><span class="hint">Books, talks, data sources</span></span></summary>
        <div class="part-body">
          <h3>Foundational and further reading</h3>
          <ul class="refs">
            <li>Balkep: The Polyculture Project<br><small><a href="https://www.thepolycultureproject.com/" rel="noopener">thepolycultureproject.com</a></small></li>
            <li>Crawford, Martin (2010): <i>Creating a Forest Garden</i>. Green Books<br><small>German: <i>Einen Waldgarten erschaffen</i> (2022), OLV</small></li>
            <li>Jacke, Dave &amp; Toensmeier, Eric (2005): <i>Edible Forest Gardens</i>, vol. 1 and 2. Chelsea Green</li>
            <li>Jacke, Dave: Reframing and Deepening into Guilds and Polycultures (FFFG1)<br><small><a href="https://www.agroforestry.co.uk/symposium/" rel="noopener">Symposium – The Agroforestry Research Trust</a></small></li>
            <li>Jacke, Dave: Gardening Like a Forest – Applied Ecological Design for Homesteads<br><small><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IL4VCbgCGD4" rel="noopener">YouTube</a></small></li>
