            <li>Hemenway, Toby (2009): <i>Gaia's Garden: A Guide to Home-Scale Permaculture</i>. Chelsea Green (chapter 9)</li>
            <li>Holmgren, David: Essence of Permaculture (German edition)<br><small><a href="https://holmgren.com.au/downloads/Essence_of_Pc_DE.pdf" rel="noopener">PDF</a></small></li>
            <li>Müller, Jörn: Ein Designprozess für Waldgärten (a design process for forest gardens), talk in German<br><small><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5Y307puQaHo" rel="noopener">YouTube</a></small></li>
            <li>Müller, Jörn: Pop goes the canopy – a forest garden design tool<br><small><a href="https://www.agroforestry.co.uk/presentations-2023/" rel="noopener">Agroforestry Research Trust, presentations 2023</a></small></li>
            <li>Müller, Jörn: polyculture for food under dry conditions (German)<br><small><a href="https://permakultur-plattform.de/blog/polykultur-fur-den-nahrungsertrag-unter-trockenen-bedingungen/" rel="noopener">Permakultur-Plattform</a></small></li>
            <li>Müller, Jörn: polyculture for food under shady conditions (German)<br><small><a href="https://permakultur-plattform.de/blog/polykultur-fuer-den-nahrungsertrag-schattig/" rel="noopener">Permakultur-Plattform</a></small></li>
            <li>Toensmeier, Eric: Guidelines for Perennial Polyculture Design<br><small><a href="https://www.permaculturenews.org/2016/01/15/guidelines-for-perennial-polyculture-design/" rel="noopener">permaculturenews.org</a></small></li>
            <li>Source code of the software<br><small><a href="https://github.com/js32/Perma-Guild-Forge" rel="noopener">github.com/js32/Perma-Guild-Forge</a></small></li>
          </ul>
          <h3 id="en-quellen">Data sources</h3>
          <dl>
            <dt>Plants For A Future</dt><dd><a href="https://pfaf.org" rel="noopener">pfaf.org</a> – the database is also good for a detailed search for useful plants.</dd>
            <dt>Wikidata</dt><dd><a href="https://www.wikidata.org" rel="noopener">wikidata.org</a> – names, images, taxonomy.</dd>
            <dt>Edible Forest Gardens</dt><dd>“Species Toolkit” species list (Lally Luck Farm), hosted as a table on our server.</dd>
            <dt>Maps</dt><dd>OpenStreetMap, official aerial photos and terrain models of the survey offices, Sentinel-2 (EOX). The source in use is shown at the bottom right of the plan.</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="small">When several sources have a value, the higher-ranked one wins (default: PFAF, then Edible Forest Gardens, then Wikidata; changeable in the <a href="/settings">settings</a>). Values you enter yourself are never overwritten. The final size of the mature plant is stored; ranges in feet are converted via their middle (25–35 ft → 9.1 m).</p>
          <h3 id="en-bildrechte">Image rights</h3>
          <p>All pictures and screenshots on this page: Perma Design Kit and Permagruen, licence <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/deed.en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="part" id="en-projekt">
        <summary><span class="num">7</span><span><span class="title">The project</span><span class="hint">Who is behind it, where the idea comes from, where it is going</span></span></summary>
        <div class="part-body">
          <p>The Perma Design Kit is a free tool for planning forest gardens and other permanent food plant systems. The software performs a niche analysis: it gathers data on a plant’s ecological niche from open sources and presents it in different ways. Besides info cards and canopy cards for laying out, it shows plants in filterable lists and calendars. As a next step, planting plans are created in 2D and 3D. Everything runs in the browser, without an account, and the data stays on your device.</p>
          <p><b>Our motivation:</b> helping people create forest gardens and other lasting land use systems. In doing so we weigh our ecological footprint against our handprint. This leads to a pragmatism that, for us, also justifies using AI for programming.</p>
          <p>The Perma Design Kit wants to be part of a cultural shift – towards a gift economy and a different way of relating to nature. It is a free tool that belongs to everyone: given and passed on, not sold.</p>

          <details class="sub" id="en-vision"><summary>Vision</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>To be part of a cultural shift – towards a gift economy and a caring relationship with nature. To that end we provide a tool that contributes to this shift.</p>
            <p><b>The tool for it:</b> people plan polycultures and forest gardens by the plants’ ecological functions, uses and site – from the plant profile through the forest garden plan to planting outside. It should help wherever planning and planting happen: at the desk, in the field, and in groups, associations and municipalities.</p>
            <p><b>Gift economy:</b> we see the kit as a gift, not a commodity. We pass it on like seeds or cuttings – without asking a price or anything in return – and trust that what sustains the project will come back: support, knowledge, plant data, translations, shared plans and examples. This ties in with the third ethic of permaculture, the fair sharing of surplus (fair share), and with the growing culture of the commons.</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-werte"><summary>Values</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>Seven values carry the project; each has a consequence by which it can be checked in everyday practice.</p>
            <div class="values">
              <div class="value"><h4>Commons</h4><p>The tool stays free and complete for everyone. Whatever is funded through support benefits everyone.</p><p class="proof">Every version becomes MIT after two years (see <a href="#lizenz">licence</a>).</p></div>
              <div class="value"><h4>Giving <span class="soon">proposal</span></h4><p>We pass on tool, plans and knowledge without asking anything in return, and invite everyone to give back what they can.</p><p class="proof">Support is never a condition; knowledge, plant data, translations and shared examples count as giving back too.</p></div>
              <div class="value"><h4>Non-commercial</h4><p>Money comes from people and bodies who want the commons – not from selling the tool.</p><p class="proof">No ads, no selling of data, no paid features, no mandatory account.</p></div>
              <div class="value"><h4>Digital sovereignty &amp; privacy</h4><p>By default the data stays with the people using it. Everything lives in the browser first; server storage, sharing and sync are optional. What the server only converts (QGIS, PFAF) it does not store.</p><p class="proof">A distinguishing feature compared with commercial planning apps.</p></div>
              <div class="value"><h4>Openness</h4><p>Disclose costs, make the origin of every plant value visible, maintain the plant data as an open data set.</p><p class="proof">Source per field (PFAF, EFG, Wikidata, manual); a page “What the project costs” is planned.</p></div>
              <div class="value"><h4>Usable by all, accessible</h4><p>A tool for a commons has to be usable without prior knowledge, without a mouse and in several languages.</p><p class="proof">Simple mode, keyboard, contrast, German and English, more languages through the community.</p></div>
              <div class="value"><h4>Respect for sources and authors</h4><p>Third-party data only with clear permission; authors are credited.</p><p class="proof">NaturaDB stays off (licence and robots.txt unresolved); Sebastian Schucht and Jens Steger are named in README and NOTICE.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-lizenz"><summary>Licence: Fair Source (FSL-1.1-MIT)</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>Since 4 October 2026 the Perma Design Kit has been licensed under the Functional Source License 1.1 with MIT future licence. Licensors are Andreas Linder and Jörn Müller.</p>
            <h4>What it allows</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Using, copying, modifying and distributing the code – for any purpose except a competing commercial offering (offering the tool or something very similar for money).</li>
              <li>Expressly allowed: internal use, education and research, consulting, running your own free instance.</li>
              <li>Plant cards, PDFs and garden plans someone creates with the app belong to them – for commercial purposes too.</li>
              <li>Every version automatically becomes MIT two years after release.</li>
              <li>Versions from before the switch and the PowerShell code in <code>legacy/</code> remain MIT.</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Why this licence</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>It protects the commons from someone taking the free tool and selling it against us as a paid product.</li>
              <li>It allows everything that contributes to the cultural shift: learning, consulting, planning – associations and municipalities can run it themselves.</li>
              <li>After two years every version becomes fully free (MIT). So the protection only covers the newest development; the knowledge stays open for good.</li>
              <li>It sells nothing: there is no licensing business. That fits a later non-profit body.</li>
            </ul>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-ziele"><summary>Goals</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p class="small">As of 1 November 2026</p>
            <ol class="timeline">
              <li><b>Now</b><span>(weeks) Beta test and feedback from more people. Presentation at the FFFG26 conference. Funding the project: settle a body to receive support. Fixing bugs, a performance test with every deploy.</span></li>
              <li><b>Next</b><span>(1–2 months) Check the benefits with the people using it and draw up a roadmap for further development.</span></li>
              <li><b>Then</b><span>(from 3 months) Build ideas from the workshop list.</span></li>
            </ol>
            <p><b>Spreading it</b> – where forest gardens are planned: shared example projects and models, permaculture and agroforestry networks, community-supported agriculture, talks (e.g. FFFG26), newsletter, public plant profiles.</p>
          </div></details>

          <details class="sub" id="en-worte"><summary>Words we use</summary><div class="sub-body">
            <p>Language shapes how people experience the project. These words fit the picture of a gift economy (the list was written for German usage):</p>
            <ul class="words">
              <li><s>product, offering</s><b>tool, commons</b><span>It belongs to everyone and is not traded.</span></li>
              <li><s>customers, users</s><b>gardeners, community, contributors</b><span>People are part of the project, not buyers.</span></li>
              <li><s>sell, offer</s><b>give, pass on, share</b><span>For what the project gives. Money that comes in we do not call a “gift” (tricky for tax).</span></li>
              <li><s>donation, payment</s><b>contribution, support</b><span>Giving back voluntarily without anything in return; “donation” only once there is a non-profit body.</span></li>
              <li><s>price, fee</s><b>pay-what-you-can contribution</b><span>For workshops and courses.</span></li>
              <li><s>sponsoring member</s><b>also: co-carriers, circle of supporters</b><span>People who carry the commons.</span></li>
              <li><s>growth, reach</s><b>spreading, passing on, sowing</b><span>The tool spreads like seed.</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p>More fitting words: gift, abundance instead of scarcity, reciprocity, culture of contribution, commoning, sharing surplus, cuttings, seed swap.</p>
          </div></details>

          <h3>How the tool came about</h3>
          <ol class="timeline">
            <li><b>2023</b><span>Jörn Müller presents the tool at the international forest garden symposium under the title “Pop goes the canopy – a forest garden design multitool”. It grew out of his consulting for forest gardens on community land and from developing courses.</span></li>
            <li><b>2024/25</b><span>Sebastian Schucht contacts Jörn; their collaboration produces a first version as a PowerShell script that generates canopy cards to scale.</span></li>
            <li><b>2025/26</b><span>Andi Linder takes over development, Sebastian leaves.</span></li>
            <li><b>2026</b><span>Jens Steger drives development forward with AI and further ideas on architecture, usability and server. The result is a web app with polycultures, calendars and the forest garden plan.</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="small">Photos and graphics on this page: from the project documentation by Jörn Müller (permagrün).</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

